From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Sat, 25 Jun 94 09:23:54 EDT
Subject: [#94-6-141] State Schooling 

I support private schools.  My support is tempered by first hand knowledge
obtained by attending some of the most highly ranked private schools in the
US: Greenwich Country Day School, Phillips Academy at Andover, Duke and
Yale.  Private schools are not a panacea and my many experiences at these
august institutions have been decidedly mixed.  Some of my best learning
experiences occurred at a public elementary school where a teacher 
voluntarily provided me with the equivalent of private tutoring.

One may argue that privatizing education is desirable because the state will
be reduced in size and power but I personally think it is naive to expect a
significant change in the overall quality of education.

Tim Starr writes:
>Classrooms of 50 students were common in the 1950s,
>when test scores were much higher - according to the SATs, the average
>score was 75 points higher then, 500 out of 800.  The recent change was
>to renormalize today's average of about 425 back up to 500.

Mentioning this change in SAT scores is misleading. The implication is that
the change in SAT scores indicates that the average student is getting worse.
The situation is more complicated.  Why use a flawed statistical methodology 
when better information is available?

As I mentioned in an earlier message I suggest checking out:

What's Really behind the SAT Score Decline
by Charles Murray and R. J. Herrnstein
The Public Interest
Winter 1992

They conclude that the average student today is no worse than in the 50s and
60s.  This does not mean the average student is a ``good'' student. 

Tim Starr also mentions changes in the ``elite'' students.  He quotes a book
which mentions the decline in the number of students obtaining high verbal
scores on the SAT.  He may be unaware of the full story. The number of
students obtaining high (above 700) scores in math has actually increased
since 1972.

He is another excerpt from the Murray & Herrnstein article:

The best and the brightest

One more piece of the puzzle that needs to be put in place.  The SAT
population constitutes a "broad elite", encompassing much of the upper
quartile of the annual national pool of academic ability. But it must be
remembered what a modest score it takes to join this pool.  Surprising as
it may seem to most readers, an SAT verbal score of just 500 puts a student
in the top 15 percent of the distribution for high school graduates as 
a whole. What has been happening to the scores of the "narrow elite,"
the most gifted students, the ones who must be counted upon to fill the 
nation's graduate and professional schools?

Daniel J. Singal has explored this question in a November 1991 Atlantic
article, "The Other Crisis in American Education."  He documents a 40 
percent drop in the proportion of students scoring over 600 in the verbal 
test, from 11.4 percent in 1972 to 6.9 percent in 1983, and the 
deterioration in the SAT scores in freshmen at the nation's selective
colleges.

Yet the news is not all bad.  For the math test, the proportion of students
scoring over 600 dropped in the 1970s but had returned to the 1972 level
by the mid-1980s.  Singal credits an increasing Asian-American proportion
of test-takers for this positive trend, but further examination suggests
otherwise.  The figures for whites only, available since 1980, show 
virtually the same degree of improvement in the proportion scoring over 600
as shown by the national figures.

When we focus on the performance of an even narrower elite of students
scoring over 700, the story is still more encouraging.  We see the same 
substantial decline in math scores from 1972 to 1981, but then the scores 
rebounded phenomenally.  In just ten years since the low point in 1981,
the proportion of students scoring over 700 in the math test has risen
by two-thirds.  The proportion scoring over 700 in 1991 was 24 percent
higher than it was for the first available figure in 1972.  Once again,
this improvement is not explained by increases by Asian test-takers.  It
holds true for the whites-only data as well.

The story for the 700+ scorers in the SAT-verbal, however, is similar
to the depressing story we see in the overall SAT population and the
one that Singal reports for the 600+ group.  The proportion of students
attaining 700 or higher in the verbal SAT fell sharply from 1967 to
the mid-1970s.  But unlike the math scores, no rebound occurred.  The
proportional size of the drop, 40 percent, was large.

The experience of the narrow elite of American students, the top few
percentiles of the overall distribution, constitutes one of the most
intriguing parts of the puzzle posed by the trends in SAT scores.  What kind
of educational system can produce such a marked response among the most
talented in the skills measures by the math test and at the same time
produce continued deterioration among the most talented in the skills
measured by the verbal test?

End of excerpt

About the authors: Charles Murray is Bradley fellow at the American
Enterprise Institute. R. J. Herrnstein is Edgar Pierce Professor of 
Psychology at Harvard University.





