From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Sat, 25 Jun 94 12:26:03 EDT
Subject: [#94-6-143] Scenarios: What happens when nanotechnology appears?

  The scenarios that Drexler, Peterson and Pergamit describe in Unbounding
the Future are predominantly fun and attractive.  A congressman could be
enticed to support nanotechnology based on the scenarios given in the book -
which may be the real point of the book.  Unfortunately, the vision of the
future given in the book is primarily unrealistically sanguine. I believe
with some sadness that the most likely occurrence after the development of
nanotechnology will be a total war with the potential for massive
devastation.

Total war will lead to a several interesting and/or appalling possibilities.
Consider the following five visions:

1) All humans and all sentient entities with human-level cognitive
capabilities (robots, genetically engineered creatures etc.) will be
annihilated.  Human-level sentient life will have to re-evolve.

2) One being will emerge with sufficient power to completely subordinate all
other beings. All other beings will be eradicated, contained, suspended, made
into pets, or rendered harmless in some other way.  The controlling entity
will function as a single massively distributed creature which spans the
galaxy and ultimately the universe.  To achieve this goal it will create
strictly subordinate replicating entities which will colonize the universe.

3) A small number of beings will emerge to form an oligarchy of power.  The
oligarchy will be stable or shift slowly. Alternatively, the oligarchy will
collapse and scenario two will emerge.  Each individual entity would
probably be distributed over major regions of the universe.  The entities
may be locked in a stalemated war.  This is a very complex scenario.  The
developments in this scenario would be difficult to predict.  

4) Large numbers of entities of varying powers and capabilities will inhabit
many worlds and will interact in a pure anarchocapitalist economy. The
entities will create an enormous market by freely and cooperatively
exchanging goods and services. The economists in this society will
enthusiastically discuss how this arrangement has lead to the greatest
happiness for all.

5) An eden-like socialistic society spanning many worlds will be created
where humans and human derivatives frolic joyfully. The self-described
wonderful and wise central planners will irrepressibly discuss how they are
able to assure the happiness of all beings without oppression.

Note, I am not specifically championing one of these scenarios.  You may
perceive a satirical tinge to some descriptions.

What future scenarios do you envision?  Perhaps you think total war is
avoidable? Other scenarios I have read about are:

6) Drexler/Miller blue goo scenario.

7) Moravec's scenario given in the chapters he posted to the extropian list
many months ago.  I do not know what scenario he discussed at the extropian
conference.

     Gregory Sullivan

