> 
> In the Middle Ages, students were expected to have mastered not only reading,
> writing, and counting, but also grammar, logic, and rhetoric - the trivium,
> or first three, of the liberal arts.  Upon entering college at the age of 16,
> they then studied the quadruvium: history, literature, philosophy, and
> mathematics.  For their texts, they took what some now call the Great Books
> of Western thought in each subject, such as Herodotus, Homer, Aristotle,
> and Euclid.  Many students now leave college without having encountered any
> of these, or their equivalents from non-Western cultures.
> 
> State schooling is dead, because it is inherently incapable of imparting to
> students the high skills which are earning the fastest-growing rewards of
